purpose operation multipartFileBytesHandler "Echo the uploaded file bytes from multipart `upload` with that part's reported content type, or 400 when the part is absent"
invariant operation multipartFileBytesHandler "Success-leg response length comes from http.multipartPartLength (not strlen on uploadFileBytes), so file bytes are not truncated at embedded NUL bytes — the round-trip preserves arbitrary binary. The content type comes from the part's declared Content-Type and is passed back as the response Content-Type. http.multipartPartContentType returns NULL when the part exists but has no Content-Type header; the native ss_http_response_bytes runtime defaults a NULL contentType to `application/octet-stream`, so no pointer.isNull guard is needed here — but a future refactor that uses the value for anything OTHER than passing straight to http.responseBytes MUST add a guard. The gauntlet's multipart fixture sends an explicit Content-Type, so the test assertion (expected_content_type=`application/x-gauntlet`) verifies the non-NULL path; a separate fixture without the part header would exercise the NULL → default path."
warning multipartFileBytesHandler "The 400 leg uses http.responseText for plain-text error reporting; this intentionally differs from the success leg's http.responseBytes path. Reusing http.responseBytes for the error leg would require constructing a body length for a static string and risk content-type ambiguity in the error response."

